# Python testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' testing guidance to `pytest`. Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files; see `skills/python-testing/SKILL.md` for the test-authoring workflow this rule file backs.

## Layout and discovery

- Match the project's layout: a `tests/` tree mirroring `src/`, or co-located `test_*.py`/`*_test.py`; do not introduce the other alongside it.
- Name tests so `pytest`'s default discovery finds them (`test_*.py`/`*_test.py`, `Test*` classes with no `__init__`, `test_*` functions), not via `testpaths` overrides.
- Keep a fixture in the narrowest `conftest.py` that covers every test needing it; do not hoist a single-file fixture to the suite root.

## Fixtures and parametrization

- Default to `function` scope; widen to `module`/`session` only for expensive, read-only setup, never for a fixture that mutates shared state.
- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` when only inputs vary; use a fixture (or `pytest.fixture(params=[...])`) when setup/teardown varies.
- Give parametrized cases explicit `ids=` when the values are not self-describing.

## Mocking

- Patch at the point of use (`mocker.patch("mypkg.module.dependency")`), not at the definition site.
- Use `unittest.mock.AsyncMock` (or `mocker.patch` with `new=AsyncMock()`) for an `async def` dependency, not a plain `Mock`.

## Async tests

- Mark async tests with `pytest.mark.asyncio` (or the project's configured async plugin/mode).
- Use the project's configured event-loop fixture scope; do not create a new loop per test unless the plugin requires it.

## Determinism

- Never depend on real wall-clock time, network access or filesystem state outside `tmp_path`; freeze time (`freezegun` or equivalent) and mock the network.
- Do not depend on dict/set iteration order beyond the guaranteed dict insertion order; sort before comparing.

## Coverage expectations

- Every new or touched public function/class/endpoint gets at least one test covering its happy path, documented edge cases and raised exception types.
- A test asserts one behavior; split a test with several unrelated assertions.
- Run the configured coverage tool (`pytest --cov`) and close uncovered branches in touched code; no `# pragma: no cover` on real logic.
